window.QUIZ_BANK['mobile-testing'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['mobile-testing']||[]).concat([
{id:2401,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What are the main types of mobile app testing?',options:['Only functional and performance testing','Unit, integration, functional, performance, security, usability, and compatibility testing','Only UI testing and API testing','Only manual and automated testing'],answer:1,explanation:'Mobile testing encompasses multiple types: unit tests validate individual components, integration tests validate interactions between components, functional tests validate features, performance tests validate speed and resource usage, security tests find vulnerabilities, and usability tests evaluate user experience.'},
{id:2402,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the Android testing pyramid?',options:['A cloud testing hierarchy','A model recommending more unit tests at the base, fewer integration tests in the middle, and fewest end-to-end/UI tests at the top','A tool for organizing Android test suites','A performance testing framework'],answer:1,explanation:'The Android testing pyramid recommends ~70% unit tests (fast, isolated, no device needed), ~20% integration tests (interact with the system but not full UI), and ~10% UI/end-to-end tests (slow, require a device). This balance optimizes feedback speed and test maintenance cost.'},
{id:2403,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between instrumentation tests and local unit tests in Android?',options:['They are identical','Local unit tests run on the JVM without a device and are faster; instrumentation tests run on an Android device/emulator and can access Android framework APIs','Local unit tests run on a device; instrumentation tests run on the JVM','Instrumentation tests only test the UI'],answer:1,explanation:'Local unit tests (in src/test/) run on the developer\'s JVM without needing a device, executing in milliseconds. Instrumentation tests (in src/androidTest/) run inside an Android process on a real device or emulator, allowing access to Android Context, Activities, and framework APIs.'},
{id:2404,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is JUnit 4 and how is it used in Android testing?',options:['A mobile UI testing framework','A Java unit testing framework used as the foundation for both local and instrumented Android tests, providing @Test, @Before, @After, and @Rule annotations','An Android-specific testing library not related to JUnit','A performance benchmarking tool'],answer:1,explanation:'JUnit 4 is the standard Java testing framework used in Android. It provides @Test to mark test methods, @Before/@After for setup/teardown, @BeforeClass/@AfterClass for one-time setup, and @Rule for test behavior modification. AndroidJUnitRunner executes JUnit 4 tests on-device.'},
{id:2405,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Espresso testing framework?',options:['A third-party tool for Android performance testing','Google\'s official Android UI testing framework that runs instrumented tests inside the app process, providing synchronized UI interactions without explicit waits','A REST API testing framework for Android','A unit testing framework that runs on the JVM'],answer:1,explanation:'Espresso is Google\'s official Android UI testing framework. It runs as an instrumentation test inside the app\'s process, uses an IdlingResource mechanism to automatically synchronize with background operations, and provides a fluent API: onView().perform().check() for interacting with and asserting on UI elements.'},
{id:2406,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are the three core Espresso components for writing a UI test?',options:['find, click, assert','onView() (matcher), perform() (action), check() (assertion)','locator, action, verify','select, interact, validate'],answer:1,explanation:'Espresso\'s API consists of: onView(matcher) to find a UI element using a Hamcrest matcher; perform(action) to interact with it (e.g., click(), typeText()); and check(assertion) to verify its state (e.g., matches(isDisplayed()), matches(withText("Expected"))). This three-part structure forms every Espresso test interaction.'},
{id:2407,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the Espresso ViewMatchers class provide?',options:['Actions to perform on views','Matchers for finding views in the UI hierarchy, such as withId(), withText(), withContentDescription(), isDisplayed(), and isEnabled()','Assertions for verifying view states','The test runner configuration'],answer:1,explanation:'ViewMatchers provides static factory methods returning Hamcrest Matchers for locating views: withId(R.id.button) by resource ID, withText("Login") by text content, withHint(), withContentDescription(), isDisplayed(), isEnabled(), isChecked(), etc. These are passed to onView() to find the target element.'},
{id:2408,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does Espresso\'s ViewActions class provide?',options:['Matchers for finding UI elements','Actions that can be performed on views, such as click(), doubleClick(), longClick(), typeText(), replaceText(), scrollTo(), swipeLeft(), and pressKey()','Assertions to verify view state','Test lifecycle callbacks'],answer:1,explanation:'ViewActions provides static factory methods for UI interactions: click(), doubleClick(), longClick(), typeText(), replaceText(""), clearText(), scrollTo(), swipeLeft(), swipeRight(), swipeUp(), swipeDown(), pressKey(), closeSoftKeyboard(). These are passed to the perform() method.'},
{id:2409,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does Espresso\'s ViewAssertions class provide?',options:['Matchers for finding views','Actions for interacting with views','Assertions for verifying view state, most commonly matches(hamcrestMatcher) and doesNotExist()','Test reporting utilities'],answer:2,explanation:'ViewAssertions has two primary factory methods: matches(Matcher<? super View>) which verifies the view matches a given state (e.g., isDisplayed(), withText()), and doesNotExist() which asserts the view is not present in the hierarchy. These are passed to the check() method.'},
{id:2410,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is an Espresso IdlingResource and why is it important?',options:['A way to slow down tests intentionally','A mechanism to tell Espresso when the app is busy with background work (e.g., network calls, database operations), causing Espresso to wait before executing the next interaction, preventing test flakiness','A way to mock network responses','A resource manager for test data'],answer:1,explanation:'Espresso\'s synchronization model polls IdlingResources to know when the app is "idle". Without this, Espresso would interact with UI while background operations are still running, causing flaky tests. You register custom IdlingResources (or use third-party ones like OkHttp3IdlingResource) for app-specific async operations.'},
{id:2411,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you test navigation between Activities in Espresso?',options:['By using driver.navigate()','By using Intents with Espresso-Intents library: intended() to verify an Intent was sent, and intending() to stub Intents and return mock results','By directly calling Activity.startActivity()','By using the ActivityTestRule flag navigateToActivity'],answer:1,explanation:'Espresso-Intents (espresso-intents artifact) provides intending(matcher).respondWith(result) to stub outgoing intents (preventing actual navigation to other apps) and intended(matcher) to verify that a specific intent was fired. This is essential for testing navigation and external app interactions in isolation.'},
{id:2412,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What annotation is used to specify the test runner in an Android instrumentation test?',options:['@TestRunner','@RunWith(AndroidJUnitRunner.class)','@InstrumentationRunner','@AndroidTest'],answer:1,explanation:'@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4.class) (or implicitly with AndroidJUnitRunner configured in build.gradle) specifies that JUnit 4\'s Android-aware runner should execute the test. This enables @Rule usage, proper lifecycle handling, and access to InstrumentationRegistry.'},
{id:2413,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure AndroidJUnitRunner as the test runner in an Android project?',options:['In the AndroidManifest.xml','In the app\'s build.gradle under android > defaultConfig > testInstrumentationRunner','In the settings.gradle file','In a separate runner.xml file'],answer:1,explanation:'In build.gradle (app module): android { defaultConfig { testInstrumentationRunner "androidx.test.runner.AndroidJUnitRunner" } }. This configures the runner used by "./gradlew connectedAndroidTest" and Android Studio to execute instrumentation tests on the connected device.'},
{id:2414,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What Gradle command runs Android instrumentation tests on a connected device?',options:['./gradlew test','./gradlew connectedAndroidTest','./gradlew runInstrumentedTests','./gradlew androidTest'],answer:1,explanation:'"./gradlew connectedAndroidTest" compiles and runs all instrumentation tests (src/androidTest/) on all connected Android devices and emulators. Results are written to build/reports/androidTests/connected/. "./gradlew test" runs local JVM unit tests only.'},
{id:2415,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What Gradle command runs Android local (JVM) unit tests?',options:['./gradlew unitTest','./gradlew test','./gradlew localTest','./gradlew jvmTest'],answer:1,explanation:'"./gradlew test" (or "./gradlew testDebugUnitTest" for the debug build variant) executes all local unit tests in src/test/ on the JVM. No device is required. Results appear in build/reports/tests/testDebugUnitTest/.'},
{id:2416,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the UI Automator testing framework in Android?',options:['A framework for unit testing Android services','Google\'s cross-app UI testing framework that can interact with system UI and other apps, unlike Espresso which is limited to a single app','A framework for testing Android background services','A performance testing framework'],answer:1,explanation:'UI Automator (androidx.test.uiautomator) is a cross-app testing framework that can interact with the Android system UI (notifications, settings) and multiple apps. Unlike Espresso, it works outside a single app\'s process. It uses UiDevice and UiObject2 for element interaction.'},
{id:2417,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is UiDevice in UI Automator and how is it obtained?',options:['A virtual device class','A class that provides access to the Android device hardware (back button, home, recent apps, screen rotation) and is obtained via UiDevice.getInstance(InstrumentationRegistry.getInstrumentation())','The UiAutomator test runner','A UI element representation class'],answer:1,explanation:'UiDevice is the central UI Automator class for device-level operations: pressing system keys (pressBack(), pressHome()), rotating the screen, taking screenshots, and finding elements. It is a singleton obtained via UiDevice.getInstance(InstrumentationRegistry.getInstrumentation()).'},
{id:2418,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What selector does UI Automator use to find elements?',options:['By.id(), By.text(), By.desc() using the By class','UiSelector().resourceId(), UiSelector().text() using the UiSelector class (now also By class in UiAutomator 2.0)','XPath expressions','CSS selectors'],answer:1,explanation:'UI Automator 2.0 (UiAutomator2) uses the By class for selectors: By.res(packageName, id), By.text("string"), By.desc("content-desc"), By.clazz(ClassName), By.pkg(packageName). The older UiSelector class is also supported for backward compatibility.'},
{id:2419,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is Android Debug Bridge (ADB)?',options:['A UI automation framework','A versatile command-line tool that allows communication between a development machine and a connected Android device, enabling app installation, debugging, log viewing, and shell access','A performance monitoring tool','An Android emulator management tool'],answer:1,explanation:'ADB (Android Debug Bridge) is a client-server program with three components: an ADB client (CLI on the development machine), an ADB daemon (adbd, running on the device), and an ADB server (background process managing communication). It provides shell access, file transfer, app management, and log streaming.'},
{id:2420,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which ADB command allows you to execute shell commands directly on the Android device?',options:['adb run-as','adb shell','adb exec','adb terminal'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell" opens an interactive shell on the device, or "adb shell <command>" executes a single command. This enables file system access, querying app info (pm list packages), controlling app processes (am start, am force-stop), checking system state, and running any standard shell utility available on the device.'},
{id:2421,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What ADB command captures a screenshot and saves it on the device?',options:['adb screencap','adb shell screencap /sdcard/screenshot.png','adb screenshot','adb capture-screen'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell screencap /sdcard/screenshot.png" takes a screenshot and saves it to the device\'s storage. You then pull it with "adb pull /sdcard/screenshot.png ./local_screenshot.png". Some CI/CD scripts use this for test failure documentation.'},
{id:2422,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What ADB command records the screen on Android?',options:['adb shell record','adb shell screenrecord /sdcard/video.mp4','adb record-screen','adb shell capture-video'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell screenrecord /sdcard/video.mp4" records the screen for up to 3 minutes (default) at 4Mbps. Options include --size, --bit-rate, and --time-limit. The video file must then be pulled from the device with "adb pull". This is useful for CI test failure debugging.'},
{id:2423,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of Android Studio\'s Layout Inspector during testing?',options:['It runs Espresso tests','It provides a real-time visual hierarchy of the running app\'s UI, showing view properties, bounds, and tree structure, helping identify element selectors for test automation','It profiles app CPU usage','It manages connected test devices'],answer:1,explanation:'Android Studio\'s Layout Inspector (View > Tool Windows > Layout Inspector) connects to a running app and displays an interactive 3D or 2D view hierarchy. Clicking on elements shows their properties (IDs, text, bounds), making it invaluable for identifying locators when writing Espresso or Appium tests.'},
{id:2424,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Robolectric framework?',options:['A framework for testing Android apps in the cloud','A unit testing framework that runs Android tests on the JVM without a device by simulating the Android framework, making tests much faster than instrumentation tests','A UI testing framework for Android','A framework for testing Android NDK code'],answer:1,explanation:'Robolectric allows Android unit tests to run on the JVM by providing a shadow implementation of the Android SDK. This makes tests extremely fast (no device needed), but shadows may not perfectly replicate all device behaviors. It bridges the gap between pure JVM unit tests and full instrumentation tests.'},
{id:2425,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you add Robolectric to an Android project?',options:['Add it to androidTest dependencies only','Add testImplementation "org.robolectric:robolectric:x.x.x" to build.gradle and annotate test classes with @RunWith(RobolectricTestRunner.class)','Add it to the app\'s main dependencies','Add it as a Gradle plugin'],answer:1,explanation:'Add "testImplementation \'org.robolectric:robolectric:x.x.x\'" to build.gradle (app module). Annotate test classes with @RunWith(RobolectricTestRunner.class). You can then create Activities, Fragments, and use Robolectric\'s shadow classes to test UI logic without a device.'},
{id:2426,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the key difference between Robolectric tests and Espresso tests?',options:['They are identical in capability','Robolectric tests run on the JVM using shadow implementations (fast, no device), while Espresso tests run on a real device/emulator (slower, more realistic). Robolectric may miss device-specific behaviors that Espresso catches.','Espresso tests run on the JVM; Robolectric tests run on a device','Robolectric only tests unit logic; Espresso only tests database operations'],answer:1,explanation:'Robolectric runs on the JVM in seconds but uses simulated Android APIs that may not perfectly replicate device behavior. Espresso runs on a real device/emulator in the actual Android environment, providing more realistic results but at higher execution cost. Both have their place in the testing pyramid.'},
{id:2427,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is MockK and how is it used in Android testing?',options:['A mock HTTP server for Android','A Kotlin-first mocking library that provides DSL-based mock creation, stub configuration, and verification for Android unit tests','A mock Android device emulator','A mocking framework for network requests only'],answer:1,explanation:'MockK is a Kotlin coroutine-aware mocking library with a clean DSL. It supports mocking final classes by default (unlike Mockito), coroutine mocking, spies, and capturing. Usage: val mock = mockk<MyClass>(); every { mock.method() } returns value; verify { mock.method() }.'},
{id:2428,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the MockK "every" DSL used for?',options:['Iterating over test data','Defining stubbed behavior for mock method calls, e.g., every { myMock.fetchUser(1) } returns User("Alice")','Verifying that all mocked methods were called','Running every test case in a loop'],answer:1,explanation:'"every { ... } returns value" sets up a stub in MockK. It defines what the mock object returns or does when a specific method with specific arguments is called. It supports answers (returns, throws, answers with a lambda), argument matchers (any(), slot()), and coroutine equivalents (coEvery).'},
{id:2429,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the MockK "verify" DSL used for?',options:['Setting up mock behavior','Asserting that a mock method was called (or not called) with specific arguments and optionally a specific number of times','Creating mock instances','Running test assertions on real objects'],answer:1,explanation:'"verify { mockObj.method(args) }" asserts that the method was called. Add "exactly = N" or "atLeast = N", "atMost = N", or "wasNot Called" for more precise verification. "confirmVerified(mock)" ensures no unexpected interactions occurred on the mock.'},
{id:2430,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you mock a Kotlin coroutine suspend function with MockK?',options:['Regular every{} works for suspend functions','Use coEvery { suspendMock.fetchData() } returns result and coVerify { suspendMock.fetchData() } for coroutine-aware stubbing and verification','Suspend functions cannot be mocked','Use mockSuspend() helper function'],answer:1,explanation:'"coEvery { mock.suspendFunction() } returns value" is MockK\'s coroutine-aware stubbing for suspend functions. Similarly, "coVerify { mock.suspendFunction() }" verifies coroutine calls. These run within a coroutine context and work with TestCoroutineDispatcher for time control.'},
{id:2431,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of InstrumentationRegistry in Android tests?',options:['It registers test cases with the test runner','It provides access to the Instrumentation instance and the application context (getInstrumentation(), getContext(), getTargetContext()) needed for interacting with the Android system in instrumented tests','It manages test device connections','It configures the test runner'],answer:1,explanation:'InstrumentationRegistry provides static access to: getInstrumentation() (the Instrumentation instance), getContext() (the test app\'s context), and getTargetContext() (the app under test\'s context). This is essential for creating components that need Android Context in instrumentation tests.'},
{id:2432,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the ActivityScenario class in Android testing?',options:['A class for creating mock Activities','An AndroidX test class that launches and manages an Activity\'s lifecycle in a controlled way for testing, replacing the deprecated ActivityTestRule','A UI hierarchy inspector for Activities','A performance measurement tool for Activities'],answer:1,explanation:'ActivityScenario (from androidx.test.core) provides a modern, lifecycle-aware way to launch Activities in tests: ActivityScenario.launch(MyActivity::class.java). It provides onActivity{} for running code on the UI thread, moveToState() for lifecycle transitions, and works with both Robolectric and instrumented tests.'},
{id:2433,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What replaced ActivityTestRule in modern Android testing?',options:['ActivityTestRunner','ActivityScenario and the ActivityScenarioRule JUnit rule from AndroidX Test','ActivityLifecycleRule','TestActivity'],answer:1,explanation:'ActivityTestRule is deprecated. ActivityScenario (for manual lifecycle control) and ActivityScenarioRule (for automatic launch before/after each test) from androidx.test.core and androidx.test.ext.junit.rules are the modern replacements, offering better lifecycle control and compatibility with the Fragment scenario.'},
{id:2434,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the FragmentScenario class used for in Android testing?',options:['For testing Fragment navigation only','For launching a Fragment in isolation within a test container Activity, allowing unit-level testing of Fragment UI and behavior without a full host Activity','For creating mock Fragments','For measuring Fragment rendering performance'],answer:1,explanation:'FragmentScenario (androidx.fragment.app.testing) launches a Fragment in a minimal empty Activity container. This allows testing the Fragment\'s UI, state restoration, and behavior in isolation. It supports actions on the Fragment: fragmentScenario.onFragment { fragment -> ... }.'},
{id:2435,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Where should Android unit tests (JVM tests) be placed in the project structure?',options:['src/androidTest/java/','src/test/java/','src/main/java/test/','test/unit/'],answer:1,explanation:'Local unit tests that run on the JVM without a device belong in src/test/java/ (or src/test/kotlin/). Instrumentation tests that require a device go in src/androidTest/java/. Android Studio creates both directories by default when creating a new project.'},
{id:2436,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the @SmallTest, @MediumTest, and @LargeTest annotations in Android testing?',options:['They set the test execution timeout','They are size annotations from AndroidX Test that categorize tests by expected duration: Small (<200ms), Medium (<1s), Large (>1s). They can be used to filter test execution.','They define test priority','They control test parallelism'],answer:1,explanation:'@SmallTest, @MediumTest, and @LargeTest (from androidx.test.filters) annotate tests by execution duration. You can filter tests in Gradle: ./gradlew connectedAndroidTest -Pandroid.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.size=small. This allows running only fast tests during development and all tests in CI.'},
{id:2437,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you test that a specific exception is thrown in JUnit 4?',options:['@Test(shouldThrow=Exception.class)','@Test(expected = SomeException.class) or using ExpectedException rule or assertThrows()','try { ... } catch { pass() }','@Throws(SomeException.class)'],answer:1,explanation:'JUnit 4 offers: @Test(expected = Exception.class) for simple cases; ExpectedException @Rule for verifying message and cause; or the preferred org.junit.Assert.assertThrows(Exception.class, () -> { code() }) for JUnit 4.13+ which allows assertions on the caught exception.'},
{id:2438,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the @Before annotation in JUnit 4?',options:['Runs before all test methods once per class','Runs before each individual test method, used for setup operations like initializing objects, creating mocks, or setting up test fixtures','Marks the test method that runs first','Runs before the test suite'],answer:1,explanation:'@Before marks a method that runs before each @Test method in the test class. It is used for per-test setup: creating fresh instances, initializing mocks (MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(this)), starting a server, or resetting shared state. Each test gets a clean setup.'},
{id:2439,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between @BeforeClass and @Before in JUnit 4?',options:['They are identical','@BeforeClass runs once before all test methods in the class (must be static); @Before runs before each individual test method','@BeforeClass only works with parameterized tests','@Before runs before the class is loaded; @BeforeClass runs before each test'],answer:1,explanation:'@BeforeClass annotates a static method that runs once before any test in the class, used for expensive one-time setup (e.g., starting a server). @Before runs before each test method for per-test setup. The corresponding teardown annotations are @AfterClass (once) and @After (per test).'},
{id:2440,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Espresso\'s onData() used for?',options:['For testing data binding in Android','For interacting with items in AdapterViews (ListView, GridView, Spinner) where views may be recycled, using a Hamcrest matcher to find the data item rather than the view','For testing database contents','For waiting for data loading operations'],answer:1,explanation:'onData(dataMatcher) is used for AdapterView-based widgets (ListView, GridView, Spinner). Since these recycle views, onView() may not find off-screen items. onData() locates items by matching the underlying data object, causing Espresso to scroll the item into view before interaction.'},
{id:2441,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Espresso\'s onWebView() / Web.onWebView() used for?',options:['For testing WebSocket connections','For testing web content inside a WebView component within an Android app, using Web Matchers and Web Assertions from the espresso-web library','For testing Chrome browser URLs','For testing network responses in WebViews'],answer:1,explanation:'espresso-web extends Espresso to test WebView content. onWebView().withElement(findElement(Locator.ID, "login")).perform(webClick()) interacts with HTML elements inside a WebView. It uses the JavaScript Atom API to find and interact with DOM elements, enabling hybrid app testing.'},
{id:2442,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you test RecyclerView items with Espresso?',options:['Using onData() directly','Using RecyclerViewActions from espresso-contrib: onView(withId(R.id.recycler)).perform(RecyclerViewActions.actionOnItemAtPosition(0, click()))','Using standard onView().perform(click()) on list position','RecyclerView cannot be tested with Espresso'],answer:1,explanation:'espresso-contrib provides RecyclerViewActions for interacting with RecyclerView items: actionOnItemAtPosition(position, action), actionOnItem(itemMatcher, action), and scrollToPosition(). These handle RecyclerView\'s view recycling and scrolling to bring items into view before interaction.'},
{id:2443,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the Espresso @Rule annotation with ActivityScenarioRule?',options:['To mark a test as a UI test','To automatically launch a specified Activity before each test and finish it after, providing an ActivityScenario instance for the test','To set the activity orientation','To configure the test timeout'],answer:1,explanation:'@Rule public ActivityScenarioRule<MyActivity> rule = new ActivityScenarioRule<>(MyActivity.class); launches MyActivity before each test and cleans up after. rule.getScenario() provides access to the ActivityScenario for programmatic lifecycle control or UI thread execution.'},
{id:2444,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "adb shell am start" in Android testing?',options:['Starts the ADB server','Starts an Activity, Service, or sends Broadcasts on the device using the Activity Manager, useful for launching apps or specific screens during test setup','Starts the Android media server','Starts the ADB monitoring tool'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell am start -n com.example/.MainActivity" launches the specified Activity. "am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d http://example.com" opens a URL. This is used in CI scripts to launch apps, trigger deep links, or start services before running automated tests.'},
{id:2445,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you clear an Android app\'s data using ADB?',options:['adb shell rm -rf /data/data/com.example','adb shell pm clear com.example.app','adb uninstall com.example.app','adb shell clear-data com.example.app'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell pm clear com.example.app" clears all data (shared preferences, databases, cache, files) for the specified package, equivalent to "Clear Data" in device settings. This is used in test setup to ensure a clean app state without reinstalling.'},
{id:2446,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Android Profiler in Android Studio and how does it assist in testing?',options:['A code coverage tool','A real-time performance monitoring tool integrated in Android Studio that shows CPU, memory, network, and energy usage of the running app, helping identify performance regressions during testing','A UI element inspector','A tool for analyzing test results'],answer:1,explanation:'Android Profiler (View > Tool Windows > Profiler) provides real-time graphs of CPU usage, memory allocation, network traffic, and battery consumption. During testing, it helps identify memory leaks (via heap dumps), CPU bottlenecks, excessive network calls, and battery drain caused by test scenarios.'},
{id:2447,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the Android "testInstrumentationRunner" configuration and what is the default value?',options:['The Gradle task name for running tests; default is "test"','A build.gradle config specifying which test runner to use for instrumentation tests; default is "androidx.test.runner.AndroidJUnitRunner"','The class that compiles tests; default is "JavaCompiler"','The thread pool for parallel tests; default is 4'],answer:1,explanation:'testInstrumentationRunner in android { defaultConfig {} } specifies the class that runs instrumentation tests. The standard value is "androidx.test.runner.AndroidJUnitRunner". Custom runners (e.g., Hilt\'s HiltTestRunner) can be substituted for dependency injection support in tests.'},
{id:2448,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What Espresso API is used to check that a view does not exist in the hierarchy?',options:['check(isGone())','check(doesNotExist())','assertFalse(onView().isPresent())','check(notVisible())'],answer:1,explanation:'onView(matcher).check(doesNotExist()) asserts that no view matching the matcher exists anywhere in the current view hierarchy. This is different from matches(not(isDisplayed())) which asserts the view exists but is not visible. doesNotExist() is appropriate for verifying views have been removed.'},
{id:2449,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of the Espresso "FailureHandler" interface?',options:['For handling exceptions thrown during app execution','A customization point that receives control when an Espresso action or assertion fails, allowing custom error reporting (e.g., saving a screenshot) before the default exception is re-thrown','For handling network failures in tests','For filtering which tests run after a failure'],answer:1,explanation:'By implementing FailureHandler and calling Espresso.setFailureHandler(customHandler), you intercept test failures. A common use is taking a screenshot via UiDevice.getInstance().takeScreenshot() when an Espresso check fails, then delegating to the DefaultFailureHandler. This improves CI test failure debugging.'},
{id:2450,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the Espresso "Intents.init()" and "Intents.release()" method pair do?',options:['Initializes and releases the Espresso library','Starts and stops intent recording, enabling intended() verifications for intents sent during the test. Must be called before and after each test using Intents API.','Initializes and releases mock network connections','Starts and stops the test Activity'],answer:1,explanation:'Intents.init() must be called before any intent interactions to begin recording intents. Intents.release() must be called after to clear recorded intents and avoid interference with other tests. The @Rule IntentsTestRule (deprecated) or manually calling init/release in @Before/@After handles this lifecycle.'},
{id:2451,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you test a BroadcastReceiver in Android?',options:['Using Espresso onView()','By sending a broadcast with InstrumentationRegistry.getInstrumentation().sendBroadcast(intent) or using LocalBroadcastManager in tests, then verifying the receiver\'s effects','Broadcasts cannot be tested','By mocking the BroadcastReceiver class'],answer:1,explanation:'You test BroadcastReceivers by sending an Intent using Context.sendBroadcast(intent) or LocalBroadcastManager, then asserting the side effects (UI changes, database updates). For unit tests, you can directly call the receiver\'s onReceive() method with a mock Context and Intent.'},
{id:2452,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "adb shell input text" command used for in testing?',options:['Reads text from the device','Types text into the currently focused input field on the device, useful for scripting input without a test framework','Reads clipboard text','Sets the keyboard language'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell input text \'Hello World\'" types the specified text into the currently focused input field. It simulates keyboard input at the system level, bypassing app-level input handling. Special characters may need URL encoding. This is often used in CI scripts for quick input without running full instrumentation tests.'},
{id:2453,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the "@FlakyTest" annotation in Android testing?',options:['Marks a test that is expected to fail','Marks a test as known to be unreliable/intermittent. Used to quarantine flaky tests while being investigated, optionally with the tolerance parameter for expected pass rate','Skips a test during CI runs only','Marks a test for performance profiling'],answer:1,explanation:'@FlakyTest (androidx.test.filters) marks a test as known intermittently failing. The tolerance parameter (0.0 to 1.0) specifies the acceptable failure rate. Flaky tests can be filtered during test runs with command-line arguments, allowing the team to track them separately from reliable tests.'},
{id:2454,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "UiSelector" class in UI Automator and how does it differ from the "By" class?',options:['UiSelector is from UI Automator 1.x and uses method chaining like UiSelector().text("Login"); By is from UI Automator 2.0 and uses static factory methods. Both accomplish element location but have different APIs','They are identical in functionality','UiSelector works on iOS; By works on Android','UiSelector is for ListView items; By is for all elements'],answer:0,explanation:'UiSelector is the original UI Automator 1.0 selector API (new UiSelector().resourceId("id").text("Login")). By is the UI Automator 2.0 API (By.res("id"), By.text("Login")) returning BySelector objects used with UiDevice.findObject(BySelector). The By API is more concise and the current recommended approach.'},
{id:2455,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "UiAutomator" test size annotation equivalence in the Android testing pyramid?',options:['Unit tests','Integration and UI tests that cross app boundaries, categorized as @LargeTest or @MediumTest in the testing pyramid','Only performance tests','Only system tests'],answer:1,explanation:'UI Automator tests are cross-app, system-level tests that interact with multiple apps and system UI. They fall in the @LargeTest or @MediumTest category in the Android testing pyramid, slower than Espresso tests due to the cross-process IPC overhead but capable of testing complete user flows across apps.'},
{id:2456,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you grant runtime permissions to an Android app during Espresso/Instrumentation tests?',options:['Permissions are automatically granted in test environments','Use GrantPermissionRule: @Rule public GrantPermissionRule permissionRule = GrantPermissionRule.grant(Manifest.permission.CAMERA)','Call requestPermissions() in the @Before method','Permissions cannot be granted programmatically'],answer:1,explanation:'GrantPermissionRule (from androidx.test.rule) grants specified permissions before tests run: @Rule public GrantPermissionRule rule = GrantPermissionRule.grant(android.Manifest.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION). This prevents permission dialogs from appearing during UI tests and is the standard way to handle runtime permissions in instrumented tests.'},
{id:2457,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Hilt testing support for Android and how does it modify test setup?',options:['Hilt has no testing support','@HiltAndroidTest annotation + HiltAndroidRule enables Hilt dependency injection in instrumented tests. @BindValue replaces bindings for testing, and HiltTestRunner replaces AndroidJUnitRunner in build.gradle','@InjectForTest annotation injects dependencies','TestHiltComponent provides test-specific bindings'],answer:1,explanation:'Hilt testing requires: HiltTestRunner as testInstrumentationRunner, @HiltAndroidTest on test classes, HiltAndroidRule as a @Rule for injection lifecycle, and optionally @BindValue/@UninstallModules to replace production bindings with test doubles. This provides proper DI in both unit and instrumented tests.'},
{id:2458,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does "adb shell monkey" do and how is it used in testing?',options:['Tests app monkey patching','Sends a stream of pseudo-random user events (touches, gestures, system events) to the app, used for stress/stability testing to find crashes and ANRs: adb shell monkey -p com.example.app -v 500','Controls the Android monkey testing daemon','Simulates network monkey-patching'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell monkey -p <package> -v <event-count>" generates random touch, gesture, and system events. It is used for stress testing and crash discovery. Options include --seed (reproducible sequences), --throttle (delay between events), --ignore-crashes (continue after crashes), and --ignore-timeouts.'},
{id:2459,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Android Lint tool and how does it relate to testing?',options:['A performance testing tool','A static code analysis tool built into Android Studio that detects bugs, security issues, performance problems, and best practice violations in code and resources, complementing runtime tests','A runtime error detector','A test coverage analyzer'],answer:1,explanation:'Android Lint analyzes source code, XML resources, ProGuard configurations, and bytecode for potential issues without running the app. While not a testing tool per se, it complements testing by catching bugs, deprecated API usage, missing permissions, and security vulnerabilities early in the development cycle.'},
{id:2460,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is code coverage in Android testing and how do you generate it?',options:['The number of test files written','The percentage of production code lines/branches exercised by tests. Generated with "./gradlew createDebugCoverageReport" (instrumentation) or enabling testCoverageEnabled in build.gradle (JUnit/JaCoCo)','The speed at which tests execute','The total number of assertions in tests'],answer:1,explanation:'Code coverage measures what percentage of production code is executed by tests. In Android, set "testCoverageEnabled true" in build.gradle to enable JaCoCo instrumentation. Run "./gradlew createDebugCoverageReport" for instrumented tests or configure the jacocoTestReport Gradle task for unit tests to generate HTML/XML reports.'},
{id:2461,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the Espresso "closeSoftKeyboard()" action?',options:['Clears all text from the focused input','Closes the virtual keyboard after typing to prevent it from obscuring UI elements that subsequent Espresso interactions need to find or click','Disables the keyboard for the test','Switches to a different keyboard layout'],answer:1,explanation:'closeSoftKeyboard() (ViewActions) dismisses the soft keyboard after typing text. The keyboard can block views that appear below it, causing Espresso to fail when trying to interact with obscured elements. Including closeSoftKeyboard() after typeText() is a common best practice in Espresso test writing.'},
{id:2462,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "adb shell dumpsys activity" in Android testing?',options:['Displays activity source code','Outputs detailed information about the Activity Manager state, including running activities, task stacks, recent tasks, and intents, useful for understanding app state during test debugging','Dumps the activity database','Records activity performance metrics'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell dumpsys activity" provides a comprehensive dump of the Activity Manager: current focus, task back stack, activity history, and recent tasks. "adb shell dumpsys activity activities" shows the task/activity stack specifically. This helps debug navigation state issues in mobile tests.'},
{id:2463,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Android Test Orchestrator and what problem does it solve?',options:['An orchestrator for managing device connections','A component from AndroidX Test that runs each instrumented test in its own instrumentation process, preventing test pollution (shared state, singleton corruption) and ensuring each test starts with a clean application state','A CI/CD orchestration tool for Android builds','A test scheduler for parallel execution'],answer:1,explanation:'Android Test Orchestrator runs each test case in a separate app process, clearing state between tests. Without it, a crash or corrupted singleton in one test can affect subsequent tests. Enable with "testOptions { execution "ANDROIDX_TEST_ORCHESTRATOR" }" and adding the Orchestrator dependency.'},
{id:2464,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you test ViewModel in Android unit tests without a real ViewModel?',options:['ViewModels cannot be unit tested','Instantiate the ViewModel directly in unit tests, inject mock dependencies (repository, use cases) via constructor injection, use TestCoroutineDispatcher for coroutine testing, and assert LiveData/StateFlow values','Use Espresso to observe ViewModel LiveData','Use Robolectric to create a real ViewModel instance'],answer:1,explanation:'ViewModels are unit-testable by direct instantiation with mock dependencies. For LiveData, use InstantTaskExecutorRule to execute tasks synchronously. For Kotlin Flows/Coroutines, use TestCoroutineDispatcher/UnconfinedTestDispatcher. Assert LiveData values via observeForever() or use the livedata-testing library.'},
{id:2465,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the InstantTaskExecutorRule in Android testing?',options:['Runs all tasks on a background thread','A JUnit rule that replaces the default Executor used by Architecture Components (LiveData, Room) with a synchronous one that executes tasks immediately on the calling thread, making async operations testable without delays','A rule that controls test execution order','A rule that prevents threading in tests'],answer:1,explanation:'@Rule public InstantTaskExecutorRule rule = new InstantTaskExecutorRule(); makes Architecture Components execute synchronously in tests. This means LiveData observers are notified immediately when postValue() is called, eliminating the need for CountDownLatch or TestObserver patterns when testing LiveData-based ViewModels.'},
{id:2466,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "adb shell input keyevent 26" in Android test automation?',options:['Opens the app settings','Sends key event 26, which corresponds to the Power button, commonly used to lock or wake the screen during automated testing','Opens the keyboard','Sends a back navigation event'],answer:1,explanation:'Key event 26 is KEYCODE_POWER. "adb shell input keyevent 26" simulates pressing the power button, toggling between screen on and screen off states. Key event 82 unlocks the screen, 3 is Home, 4 is Back, and 187 is Recent Apps. These are essential for testing background/foreground app behavior.'},
{id:2467,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between Mockito and MockK for Android testing?',options:['They are identical','Mockito is a Java-based mocking framework that requires special configuration (MockitoExtension, inline-mock-maker) to mock final Kotlin classes; MockK is Kotlin-first, handles final classes by default, supports coroutines natively, and provides a more idiomatic Kotlin DSL','MockK is a wrapper around Mockito','Mockito is preferred for all Kotlin Android projects'],answer:1,explanation:'Mockito works well with Java and can work with Kotlin using the mockito-kotlin wrapper and inline-mock-maker for final classes. MockK is designed specifically for Kotlin, handling final classes/objects/top-level functions naturally, providing coroutine support (coEvery/coVerify), and offering a cleaner Kotlin DSL without Java interop workarounds.'},
{id:2468,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you test Room database operations in Android?',options:['Room databases cannot be unit tested','Use an in-memory Room database (Room.inMemoryDatabaseBuilder()) in tests to run database operations without a real file, combined with InstantTaskExecutorRule for LiveData/Flow and runBlocking for suspend functions','Mock all DAO interfaces','Use adb to inspect the SQLite database'],answer:1,explanation:'Room.inMemoryDatabaseBuilder(context, AppDatabase::class.java).build() creates an in-memory database that resets between test runs. Use @RunWith(AndroidJUnit4::class) for instrumented tests, runBlocking{} for suspend DAO functions, and close the database in @After. This provides real SQL validation without production database side effects.'},
{id:2469,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the "adb shell pm grant" command do?',options:['Grants developer permissions for ADB commands','Grants a runtime permission to an installed app: adb shell pm grant com.example.app android.permission.CAMERA, useful for setting up device state before automated tests','Grants root access to an app','Grants Play Store permissions'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell pm grant <package> <permission>" grants a runtime permission to the specified app. This is equivalent to the user tapping "Allow" on a permission dialog and is used in test setup scripts to ensure apps have required permissions before test execution begins, avoiding permission dialog interruptions.'},
{id:2470,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Android "StrictMode" and how can it aid in testing?',options:['A strict code style enforcer','An Android developer tool that detects accidental disk/network operations on the main thread and other violations, helping catch threading issues that could cause ANRs or test instability','A strict test assertion mode','A mode that disables animations during testing'],answer:1,explanation:'StrictMode detects violations at runtime: disk reads/writes or network calls on the main thread, leaked cursors/streams, and more. Enabling it in debug builds (or test builds) causes violations to crash the app or log warnings, helping test suites catch threading problems that would otherwise cause intermittent failures.'},
{id:2471,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are Android "test doubles" and what are the common types?',options:['Duplicate test classes','Objects that replace real dependencies in tests: Fakes (working simplified implementations), Stubs (return preset values), Mocks (verify interactions), Spies (partial mocks), and Dummies (placeholder objects that are not used)','Parallel test execution configurations','Duplicate test environments'],answer:1,explanation:'Test doubles replace production dependencies in isolation tests. Fakes have working implementations (e.g., InMemoryRepository). Stubs return hardcoded values. Mocks verify that specific interactions occurred. Spies wrap real objects and intercept calls. Dummies fill parameter slots. Choosing the right type for each scenario produces more readable, reliable tests.'},
{id:2472,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "testOptions { animationsDisabled = true }" in Android build.gradle?',options:['Disables test animations in the APK','Disables system animations (window animation scale, transition animation scale, animator duration scale) on the test device during instrumentation tests, reducing flakiness caused by animation timing issues in Espresso','Disables animations in production builds','Disables screen transitions in the test runner'],answer:1,explanation:'testOptions { animationsDisabled = true } in build.gradle automatically disables the three system animation settings during instrumented test runs. Without this, animations can cause Espresso to fail because the UI transitions take time, and Espresso may interact with elements before they reach their final animated position.'},
{id:2473,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Android Benchmark library used for in testing?',options:['For writing performance test assertions','For microbenchmarking code performance in Android. JUnit-based, it measures execution time of specific code paths (e.g., layout inflation, RecyclerView scrolling) with statistical analysis and Jetpack Macrobenchmark for app startup and screen interactions','For stress testing apps','For measuring network performance'],answer:1,explanation:'AndroidX Benchmark (androidx.benchmark:benchmark-junit4 for micro, androidx.benchmark:benchmark-macro-junit4 for macro) measures precise execution times with statistical analysis. It is run on real devices, accounts for thermal throttling and clock speed variations, and outputs results to Android Studio Benchmark Results. CI integration tracks performance regressions.'},
{id:2474,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Espresso\'s synchronization mechanism work by default?',options:['Espresso uses fixed Thread.sleep() delays','Espresso automatically synchronizes with the UI thread message queue (Looper) and AsyncTask thread pool, ensuring commands execute only when the app is idle, without needing explicit wait calls','Espresso polls the UI every 100ms','Espresso uses a custom synchronized lock'],answer:1,explanation:'Espresso\'s IdlingRegistry monitors: the UI thread\'s message queue (empty = idle), AsyncTask thread pool (all threads idle), and any registered IdlingResources. Before each interaction, Espresso waits up to the timeout (default 60s) for all registered resources to report idle, then executes the action.'},
{id:2475,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the CountingIdlingResource and when do you use it?',options:['A resource for counting test assertions','A thread-safe IdlingResource implementation that increments a counter when async work begins and decrements when it finishes. When the count is 0, Espresso considers the resource idle, correctly pausing test execution during custom async operations','A timer for test execution','A resource counter for mock objects'],answer:1,explanation:'CountingIdlingResource is the standard implementation for tracking custom async operations. Call increment() before starting background work and decrement() upon completion. Register it with IdlingRegistry.getInstance().register(resource). This integrates cleanly with coroutines (using a custom scope), OkHttp (OkHttp3IdlingResource), or any custom threading mechanism.'},
{id:2476,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "adb shell settings put" command useful for in Android test setup?',options:['Installs app settings','Changes Android system settings values programmatically, e.g., "adb shell settings put global animator_duration_scale 0" to disable animations before running tests','Stores test configuration data','Pushes settings files to the device'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell settings put" modifies system, global, or secure settings. Common test uses: "settings put global window_animation_scale 0" (disable animations), "settings put global airplane_mode_on 1" (enable airplane mode), "settings put global transition_animation_scale 0". These configure the device state without physical interaction.'},
{id:2477,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Jetpack Macrobenchmark library and how does it differ from JUnit Benchmark?',options:['They are identical','Macrobenchmark (benchmark-macro-junit4) measures app-level performance like startup time, frame rendering, and scrolling in a separate process from the app under test, capturing real user-perceived latency. JUnit Benchmark measures microbenchmarks within the app process.','Macrobenchmark only runs in CI','Macrobenchmark measures network performance'],answer:1,explanation:'Macrobenchmark uses CompilationMode to control JIT/AOT and BaselineProfile, measures startup (cold/warm/hot), and measures UI jank during scrolling via JankStats. It runs the target app as a separate instrumented process, avoiding the instrumentation overhead seen in JUnit Benchmark. Results integrate with Android Studio Profiler.'},
{id:2478,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you assert that a Snackbar is displayed in an Espresso test?',options:['Directly by asserting the Snackbar class visibility','Using onView(withId(com.google.android.material.R.id.snackbar_text)).check(matches(withText("Expected"))) since Snackbar is a View-based component with known IDs','Using onSnackbar().check()','Snackbars cannot be verified with Espresso'],answer:1,explanation:'Snackbars are standard Android Views rendered in the Coordinator Layout. Espresso can find the Snackbar\'s text view by its known resource ID (com.google.android.material.R.id.snackbar_text) and assert its text. The Snackbar action button (snackbar_action) can similarly be found and clicked.'},
{id:2479,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the role of the Android "testFixtures" source set in Gradle?',options:['A set of broken tests for documentation','A dedicated source set (src/testFixtures/) for sharing test helper code, fake implementations, and test data builders across test types (unit tests and instrumentation tests) without exposing them in production code','A folder for test configuration files','A source set for benchmark tests only'],answer:1,explanation:'testFixtures (Gradle 6.6+) provides a shared source set for test infrastructure: fake repositories, test data builders, custom matchers, and helper utilities. Code in testFixtures is available to both test and androidTest source sets via testFixturesImplementation dependency, avoiding code duplication across test types.'},
{id:2480,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "adb shell wm density" in mobile testing?',options:['Measures screen brightness','Gets or sets the display pixel density (DPI) on the device, used to test app layout behavior at different screen densities: adb shell wm density 320','Measures screen resolution','Controls WiFi signal strength'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell wm density <dpi>" changes the display density, allowing you to test app layout on different density configurations without multiple physical devices. "adb shell wm density reset" restores the original density. This helps test that layouts adapt correctly across density buckets (mdpi, hdpi, xhdpi, etc.).'},
{id:2481,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "adb shell wm size" in mobile test automation?',options:['Reports the number of connected devices','Gets or sets the display resolution: "adb shell wm size 1080x1920" changes resolution for testing different screen sizes, verifying responsive layouts without needing multiple physical devices','Reports WiFi signal strength','Controls screen orientation'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell wm size WxH" overrides the display size, useful for testing responsive layouts at different resolutions. "adb shell wm size reset" restores the original size. Combined with "adb shell wm density", this allows testing various device form factors from a single device during automated or manual testing.'},
{id:2482,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you implement a custom Espresso ViewAction?',options:['By extending the Espresso class','By implementing the ViewAction interface with getConstraints() (returning a Matcher<View> that the view must satisfy), getDescription() (returning a human-readable description), and perform(UiController, View) (executing the action)','By overriding the click() method','By using the @CustomAction annotation'],answer:1,explanation:'Implement ViewAction: getConstraints() returns a matcher the target view must satisfy (e.g., isDisplayed()); getDescription() returns "My custom action"; perform(UiController uiController, View view) executes the action (e.g., calling view.setCustomProperty()). Then use onView(matcher).perform(myCustomAction()).'},
{id:2483,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the "ApplicationProvider" class in AndroidX Test?',options:['Provides access to the test APK\'s context','Provides a generic way to get the application Context in both Robolectric and instrumented tests via ApplicationProvider.getApplicationContext<Application>(), replacing the need to check InstrumentationRegistry in each test type','Manages application lifecycle in tests','Provides mock application instances'],answer:1,explanation:'ApplicationProvider.getApplicationContext<Application>() works in both Robolectric (returns Robolectric\'s simulated Application) and instrumented tests (returns the real application Context). This makes test helpers and utilities portable across test types without conditional code.'},
{id:2484,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "DisableOnAndroidDebug" JUnit rule used for?',options:['Disables tests when running in debug mode','Disables a timeout rule when the test runs in debug mode (to allow pausing at breakpoints without tests failing due to timeout), useful when wrapping Timeout rules during development','Disables all debugging in tests','Disables the Android Debug Bridge during tests'],answer:1,explanation:'@Rule public TestRule timeout = new DisableOnAndroidDebug(Timeout.seconds(30)); applies the Timeout rule normally in CI but disables it when the app is running in debug mode (detected via Debug.isDebuggerConnected()). This allows developers to use breakpoints without hitting test timeouts.'},
{id:2485,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the "@SdkSuppress" annotation do in Android testing?',options:['Documents the minimum SDK for production','Restricts a test to run only on devices with API levels within the specified range using @SdkSuppress(minSdkVersion = 21) or @SdkSuppress(maxSdkVersion = 30), preventing tests using APIs unavailable on older devices from running','Sets the SDK version for the build','Marks a test as using SDK internals'],answer:1,explanation:'@SdkSuppress (from androidx.test.filters) annotates tests that should only run on specific API levels. @SdkSuppress(minSdkVersion = Build.VERSION_CODES.O) skips the test on devices running below API 26. This is used for tests relying on APIs only available in newer Android versions.'},
{id:2486,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the "requireActivity()" function do in Fragment testing?',options:['Returns a mock Activity','Returns the Activity that hosts the Fragment, used within Fragment tests (in onFragment{} lambda) to access the Activity instance for interaction or assertion','Creates a new test Activity','Returns the application Context'],answer:1,explanation:'Within FragmentScenario\'s onFragment{} block, fragment.requireActivity() returns the empty container Activity hosting the Fragment under test. This allows accessing the Activity\'s context, resources, or calling Activity-level methods when testing Fragment-Activity interactions.'},
{id:2487,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is screenshot testing in Android and which libraries support it?',options:['Recording screen videos only','Automated visual regression testing that captures UI screenshots and compares them against baseline images, detecting unexpected visual changes. Libraries: Shot (by Karumi), Paparazzi (Square, JVM-based), or Facebook\'s screenshot-tests-for-android','Screenshot testing is manual only','Only Espresso supports screenshot testing'],answer:1,explanation:'Screenshot/snapshot testing captures UI component renders and diffs them against approved baselines. Paparazzi (Square) renders views on the JVM without a device via Layoutlib, making it extremely fast. Shot (Karumi) runs on instrumented tests. Facebook\'s screenshot-tests-for-android uses Python for diff visualization. All integrate with CI to catch unintended UI regressions.'},
{id:2488,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the "@RequiresDevice" annotation in Android testing?',options:['Requires a physical device for production build','Marks a test to only run on physical devices, not emulators. Useful for tests that rely on hardware features like NFC, real camera, or telephony that emulators cannot reliably simulate','Requires a specific device model','Requires device root access'],answer:1,explanation:'@RequiresDevice (androidx.test.filters) instructs the test runner to skip the test when running on an emulator. This is appropriate for tests requiring real hardware: actual camera, NFC, Bluetooth behavior, precise touch input, or carrier-dependent features that emulators may not accurately simulate.'},
{id:2489,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "adb forward" in Android test automation?',options:['Forwards ADB commands to multiple devices','Creates a TCP port forwarding rule from the host machine to the Android device, used to access services running on the device from the host, e.g., "adb forward tcp:4724 tcp:4724" for Appium sessions on specific ports','Forwards logs to a remote server','Forwards device screen to the host'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb forward tcp:<host_port> tcp:<device_port>" tunnels traffic from a port on the development machine to a port on the device. For testing, this is commonly used to forward Appium\'s Chromedriver or WebDriverAgent communication ports, or to access custom server processes running inside the app during testing.'},
{id:2490,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the AndroidX Test "TestRunner" (AndroidJUnit4) provide beyond standard JUnit 4?',options:['Faster test execution through JVM optimization','Android-aware test lifecycle management, InstrumentationRegistry access, ActivityScenario/FragmentScenario support, Android-specific JUnit rules (GrantPermissionRule, ActivityScenarioRule), and integration with the AndroidX Test library ecosystem','Automatic test generation','Real-time test result streaming to Android Studio'],answer:1,explanation:'AndroidJUnit4 runner integrates JUnit 4 with Android\'s instrumentation system: provides InstrumentationRegistry context, supports @Rules like GrantPermissionRule and ActivityScenarioRule, enables ActivityScenario/FragmentScenario, and feeds results to Android Studio and Gradle. It is the standard runner for all modern Android instrumented tests.'},
{id:2491,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Espresso "Espresso.onIdle()" method used for?',options:['Pauses test execution for a fixed time','Forces Espresso to wait for all current IdlingResources to become idle before continuing, useful in rare scenarios where you need explicit sync outside of a perform/check call','Idles all background threads','Cancels pending async operations'],answer:1,explanation:'Espresso.onIdle() blocks test execution until all registered IdlingResources report idle. While Espresso normally does this automatically before each action/assertion, onIdle() provides explicit synchronization for custom scenarios, like waiting before asserting on non-View state that depends on background completion.'},
{id:2492,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of "testImplementation" vs "androidTestImplementation" in Android build.gradle?',options:['They are interchangeable','testImplementation adds a dependency only to the local unit test classpath (src/test/); androidTestImplementation adds it to the instrumentation test classpath (src/androidTest/). Using the wrong one can cause runtime ClassNotFoundException on-device','testImplementation is for debug builds; androidTestImplementation is for release builds','testImplementation is deprecated'],answer:1,explanation:'"testImplementation" (e.g., JUnit, MockK, Robolectric) adds libraries available only to src/test/ JVM tests. "androidTestImplementation" (e.g., Espresso, UI Automator, androidx.test.runner) adds libraries available only to src/androidTest/ instrumented tests. Using testImplementation for Espresso would cause it to be unavailable on-device.'},
{id:2493,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you test Android WorkManager tasks in unit tests?',options:['WorkManager cannot be unit tested','Use WorkManagerTestInitHelper.initializeTestWorkManager(context) to initialize WorkManager in test mode, then enqueue work and call TestDriver.setAllConstraintsMet() or setInitialDelayMet() to trigger execution, then verify outputs','Mock the WorkManager class entirely','Use Espresso to trigger work items'],answer:1,explanation:'WorkManagerTestInitHelper.initializeTestWorkManager(context) sets up WorkManager with a synchronous executor. TestListenableWorkerBuilder<MyWorker>(context).build().startWork().get() tests Workers directly. TestDriver allows triggering constraint/delay fulfillment. Assert work state via WorkManager.getInstance().getWorkInfoById(workId).get().state.'},
{id:2494,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Android "ApplicationTestCase" class and is it still recommended?',options:['A modern test class for application-level tests','An older JUnit 3-style test class for testing the Application class. It is deprecated in favor of using JUnit 4 with ApplicationProvider and standard AndroidX Test infrastructure','The base class for all Espresso tests','A class for performance testing'],answer:1,explanation:'ApplicationTestCase is a deprecated JUnit 3-era class that extended AndroidTestCase for testing Application subclasses. Modern Android testing uses JUnit 4 with @RunWith(AndroidJUnit4::class), ApplicationProvider.getApplicationContext(), and does not require this legacy class.'},
{id:2495,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What ADB command is used to start an Android Service during testing?',options:['adb shell service start','adb shell am startservice -n com.example/.MyService','adb shell pm startservice','adb service run com.example/.MyService'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell am startservice -n <package>/<ServiceClass>" or "adb shell am start-foreground-service" (for foreground services on Android 8+) starts a Service. This is used in test setup scripts to initialize background services before running tests that depend on service state.'},
{id:2496,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of Mockito\'s @Spy annotation in Android testing?',options:['Creates a completely mocked object','Creates a spy that wraps a real object instance, allowing you to override specific methods while delegating all others to the real implementation','Marks objects as test spies for reporting','Creates a partial mock of an interface'],answer:1,explanation:'@Spy creates a partial mock: methods you stub (doReturn(value).when(spy).method()) are overridden, while all others call the real implementation. This is useful when you want to test a class mostly with real behavior but mock one or two methods, such as overriding a network call within an otherwise real class.'},
{id:2497,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the significance of "testInstrumentationRunnerArguments" in Android build.gradle for test filtering?',options:['It sets JVM arguments for unit tests','It passes key-value argument pairs to the instrumentation runner, enabling test filtering: annotation filtering (annotation=com.example.Smoke), size filtering (size=small), and custom argument passing to test code via InstrumentationRegistry.getArguments()','It configures the test network settings','It sets the device serial for test targeting'],answer:1,explanation:'testInstrumentationRunnerArguments passes arguments to AndroidJUnitRunner: { "annotation" to "com.example.annotations.SmokeTest", "notAnnotation" to "com.example.annotations.Flaky" } filters tests. In the test, InstrumentationRegistry.getArguments().getString("key") retrieves custom arguments, enabling parameterized configuration from CI pipelines.'},
{id:2498,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "Strict Mode" in Espresso testing and how is it enabled?',options:['A mode that disables all warnings','Espresso.setFailureHandler for strict error handling','Espresso strict mode (if referenced) relates to using Espresso.onView() only after verifying prerequisites, or Hilt\'s strict component behavior — typically, "strict mode" in testing means using StrictMode in the app code to detect main-thread violations','A testing feature that prevents mock overrides'],answer:2,explanation:'In Android testing, StrictMode is an app-level feature (enabled in Application.onCreate() for debug builds) that detects main-thread violations (disk I/O, network), leaked resources, and other programming errors. When enabled during tests, it causes violations to crash the app, making tests catch threading bugs that would otherwise cause intermittent failures in production.'},
{id:2499,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does "adb shell input tap X Y" do and how is it different from Espresso interactions?',options:['They are equivalent','adb shell input tap sends a touch event at absolute screen coordinates (X, Y) at the ADB/kernel level, bypassing the app. It is less reliable for testing because it depends on screen resolution and element position, unlike Espresso which interacts via View references','adb tap is more reliable than Espresso','They use the same underlying mechanism'],answer:1,explanation:'"adb shell input tap X Y" injects a tap at pixel coordinates. This is fragile (coordinates change with screen size/resolution/orientation) and doesn\'t wait for the UI to be ready. Espresso/UI Automator interactions use View references or accessibility tree lookups, making them more reliable and independent of screen size.'},
{id:2500,topic:'mobile-testing',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Android Test Lab Orchestrator\'s "clearPackageData" option and what does it accomplish?',options:['Uninstalls the app after each test','When enabled with the Orchestrator, clears the app\'s shared state (SharedPreferences, databases, files) between each test run, ensuring complete test isolation without reinstalling the APK, reducing the likelihood of flaky tests due to leftover state','Clears the device\'s storage','Removes unused test dependencies'],answer:1,explanation:'"clearPackageData: true" in the Orchestrator configuration instructs it to call "pm clear <package>" between each test, wiping SharedPreferences, databases, and files. This provides the strongest test isolation without the overhead of reinstalling the APK, critical for preventing subtle state leakage in integration and UI test suites.'},
]);
